1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:06,495
Two debaters, one motion: pineapple belongs on pizza.

2
00:00:06,775 --> 00:00:15,463
The green side argues for it, and the red side argues against, each in a few punchy sentences, and each answering the other's last point.

3
00:00:15,742 --> 00:00:23,326
Then a judge scores them on five criteria and picks a winner, and finally the app asks you one question: did that change your mind?

4
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:27,951
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

5
00:00:28,751 --> 00:00:31,919
Models are good at producing a case for any side.

6
00:00:32,199 --> 00:00:38,463
That's useful, and it is also a little worrying, because the argument sounds confident whether or not it's right.

7
00:00:38,743 --> 00:00:45,390
A debate makes that visible: ask for both sides and you see two confident cases for opposite conclusions.

8
00:00:45,671 --> 00:00:50,375
Then there's the second question, which matters more in practice: who judges?

9
00:00:50,655 --> 00:01:00,423
Using a language model as a judge is a common way to evaluate other models, so I wanted a small, visible example of what that looks like, with all the scores on screen.

10
00:01:01,223 --> 00:01:03,359
Each speech is one small call..

11
00:01:03,639 --> 00:01:11,199
The system prompt gives the stance, asks for a new point plus a rebuttal of the opponent's last one, and bans pleasantries.

12
00:01:11,479 --> 00:01:16,591
The user message carries the debate so far, so each side can answer what the other said.

13
00:01:16,871 --> 00:01:25,847
Temperature is zero point nine, so the speeches vary from run to run, and the limit is a hundred and ten tokens, which keeps each speech short.

14
00:01:26,127 --> 00:01:29,415
In a debate of two rounds, that is four speeches.

15
00:01:30,214 --> 00:01:41,927
The judge gets the whole transcript and has to return JSON: a one to ten score for each side on logic, evidence, persuasion, clarity and rebuttal, a winner, and a one-sentence reason.

16
00:01:42,207 --> 00:01:45,927
The temperature is zero, so the judge is as steady as it gets.

17
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:49,134
The app then draws the scores as a radar chart.

18
00:01:49,415 --> 00:01:56,494
One more feature: before the debate you move a slider for how much you agree with the motion, and after the debate you move it again.

19
00:01:56,774 --> 00:01:58,646
The app shows your shift.

20
00:01:59,446 --> 00:02:02,662
The motion is: pineapple belongs on pizza.

21
00:02:02,942 --> 00:02:04,814
Two rounds.

22
00:02:05,094 --> 00:02:09,990
Before the debate I set my own opinion to four out of ten, slightly against.

23
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:12,143
Then I press Start.

24
00:02:12,422 --> 00:02:16,862
Pro opens: the sweetness contrasts with salty toppings like ham.

25
00:02:17,142 --> 00:02:22,254
And there is a clever move: tomatoes are a fruit, so why is pineapple out of bounds?

26
00:02:22,534 --> 00:02:30,766
Con answers that tomato's acidity complements a pizza while pineapple overwhelms it, and asks: would chocolate syrup belong too?

27
00:02:31,046 --> 00:02:37,886
In round two, Pro calls the chocolate syrup point a strawman, because ham pairs with pineapple and syrup doesn't.

28
00:02:38,166 --> 00:02:46,038
Con replies that pineapple hijacks the palate, and calls it, in its words, a sugar tsunami in a savory ecosystem.

29
00:02:46,318 --> 00:02:50,494
The judge scores Pro forty and Con thirty-six, and picks Pro.

30
00:02:50,774 --> 00:02:58,838
The reason it gives: Pro made stronger arguments about the balance and evolution of flavors, and countered Con's points about disruption.

31
00:02:59,118 --> 00:03:00,990
Did they change my mind?

32
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:06,382
I read the tomatoes point and thought it was good, even if it's a classic debating trick.

33
00:03:06,662 --> 00:03:08,846
I move my slider from four to five.

34
00:03:09,126 --> 00:03:10,998
Plus one point.

35
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:13,150
A tiny shift.

36
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:19,806
Second motion, one every programmer has an opinion on: tabs are better than spaces.

37
00:03:20,086 --> 00:03:23,830
This time I set my opinion to seven: I lean towards tabs.

38
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:24,974
Start.

39
00:03:25,254 --> 00:03:30,846
Pro argues that tabs save file size and let each person pick their own indentation width.

40
00:03:31,126 --> 00:03:38,686
Con says that fractures collaboration, because code looks different in every editor, while spaces look the same everywhere.

41
00:03:38,966 --> 00:03:45,614
In round two, Pro says consistency comes from agreeing on a standard, and tab widths are customizable.

42
00:03:45,894 --> 00:03:54,678
Con replies that a custom width creates an illusion of alignment, and that spaces are a pixel-perfect standard that tabs can never guarantee.

43
00:03:54,958 --> 00:03:58,270
The judge scores Con forty-four and Pro forty-two.

44
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:00,446
Con wins, narrowly.

45
00:04:00,726 --> 00:04:05,526
The reason: stronger evidence and rebuttal about universal consistency.

46
00:04:05,806 --> 00:04:07,678
Did it change my mind?

47
00:04:07,958 --> 00:04:10,502
A bit: I move from seven to six.

48
00:04:10,782 --> 00:04:12,654
Minus one point.

49
00:04:13,454 --> 00:04:18,398
Both debates ended close: four points apart in one, two in the other.

50
00:04:18,678 --> 00:04:21,102
The judge didn't give anyone a landslide.

51
00:04:21,382 --> 00:04:26,086
And the judge's one-sentence reasons mostly echo the winning side's main argument.

52
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:29,054
Also, the radar charts overlap a lot.

53
00:04:29,334 --> 00:04:39,990
With totals between thirty-six and forty-four out of fifty, every criterion averages between seven and nine, so the numbers look precise but say little about why one side won.

54
00:04:40,790 --> 00:04:44,126
Two debates prove nothing about whether the judge is fair.

55
00:04:44,406 --> 00:04:49,470
The same model plays Pro, Con and the judge, so it may favour its own style.

56
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:57,958
Pro always speaks first, which could matter, and I didn't test it: one debate went to each side, which is too little to say anything.

57
00:04:58,238 --> 00:05:03,782
The scores are one sample at temperature zero from one prompt; a different wording could shift them.

58
00:05:04,062 --> 00:05:09,270
And my opinion shifts, plus one and minus one, are just one person's reaction.

59
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:14,614
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

60
00:05:15,414 --> 00:05:24,726
Next I'd swap the sides to test for position bias, use a different model as the judge, and run each motion many times to see how often the winner flips.

61
00:05:25,006 --> 00:05:28,198
A human vote next to the AI judge would be good too.

62
00:05:28,478 --> 00:05:33,398
The code is in the description, with no key needed beyond your own Azure setup.

63
00:05:33,678 --> 00:05:39,750
If you liked this, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which motion should be on trial next.
